'' A WIDE LITERAL HELD THE SPELLING OF ITS ESCAPES, not their value.
''
'' StringLitText_ answers in BYTES and deliberately leaves an escaped literal
'' containing a unicode escape alone -- decoding the byte escapes around one and
'' dropping the wide ones would produce a literal that is neither compiler's answer --
'' so the RAW TEXT flowed through to the wide global. A 'wstring' initialised from
'' '!"AB"' held the TWELVE characters of the spelling where fbc has the two
'' characters AB. It lowered, it verified, and nothing anywhere said so.
''
'' 34 corpus files are made of it and none of them declines: wstring/trim.bas writes
'' 186 unicode escapes and lowered clean the whole time. Measured by RUNNING fbc's
'' output -- the three lengths in the source are 2, 3 and 3.
''
'' A '\uXXXX' cannot be a byte, so the model had to change: a wide global now carries
'' its CODE UNITS in a pool beside the data blocks, and 'bytes' keeps the source
'' spelling for the dump alone. Every site that turns a literal into a wide global goes
'' through one reader, so the length and the units come from the same decode -- they
'' disagreed before, because the length was the spelling's.
''
'' EXACTLY FOUR HEX DIGITS: fewer is not this escape, and a greedy run would swallow
'' the text after it. Every other escape is the byte the narrow reader gives, widened,
'' and a NUL from a numeric escape ends the literal exactly as it does there.
''
'' STILL WRONG, and recorded rather than half-fixed: a '\u' literal assigned to a
'' NARROW string. fbc makes the whole literal a WSTRING and emits
'' 'fb_WstrAssignToA_Init( &S, -1, L"AB", 0 )'; tiko-lang still treats it as narrow and
'' keeps the spelling. That is a TYPING question, not a reader one.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/wide-unicode-escape.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: global wstr $wstr0 "AB"
'' EXPECT-OUT: global wstr $wstr1 "\u3041\u3043\u3045"
'' EXPECT-OUT: global wstr $wstr2 "A\u0009B"
'' GOLDEN: expected/wide-unicode-escape.out
